  "manchester-c398-p111": {
    "title": "羅鬼女官",
    "title_en": "羅鬼女官 — provisional Manchester source reading",
    "page_kind": "caption",
    "status": "image_checked_draft",
    "ocr_raw": "女官即猓羅正妻，稱之曰耐德。編髮為髻，用青帕蒙之，多戴銀絲花貼額，耳垂大環，拖長裙三十六幅。非耐德所生，不得繼立。如嗣子幼，不能主事，耐德為女官，代理土務。",
    "transcription": "女官即猓羅正妻，稱之曰耐德。編髮為髻，用青帕蒙之，多戴銀絲花貼額，耳垂大環，拖長裙三十六幅。非耐德所生，不得繼立。如嗣子幼，不能主事，耐德為女官，代理土務。",
    "translation": "The “female official” is the principal wife of a Luoluo, called Naide. She braids her hair into a bun and covers it with a blue headcloth. She often wears silver-thread floral ornaments against her forehead, large rings hanging from her ears, and a long skirt of thirty-six panels. Anyone not born to the Naide may not succeed to the hereditary office. If the inheriting son is young and cannot direct affairs, the Naide serves as female official and administers local government business on his behalf.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft",
    "transcription_scope": "full_page_visual_pass_provisional_with_uncertain_characters",
    "text_completeness": "provisional_full_or_near_full",
    "english_completeness": "full_relative_to_chinese_draft",
    "translation_scope": "Full English rendering of extant provisional Chinese transcription in source order; unreadable OCR retained, not a human-certified translation.",
    "ocr_engine": "AI-assisted visual manuscript reading, not automated OCR",
    "source_review": "User-uploaded Manchester Chinese Crawford 398 IIIF canvas 111 (source checked 2026-11-22)",
    "notes": "依上傳曼徹斯特本原圖按竪排自右向左試錄；存疑字保留□；非人工定稿，歷史族稱反映原編繪者立場。 英文為試譯性概述，並非逐字對譯。",
    "english_kind": "source_aligned_translation_draft",
    "prior_english_summary_20261122": "The female official is the principal wife of a Luoluo and is called Naide. She wears her hair in a bun beneath a blue headcloth, silver-thread floral ornaments, large earrings, and a long skirt of thirty-six panels. Only a child born to Naide may inherit. When the heir is too young to govern, she acts as female official and administers local affairs.",
    "translation_full_draft_20261122": true
  },
  "manchester-c398-p113": {
    "title": "豬屎犵狫",
    "title_en": "豬屎犵狫 — provisional Manchester source reading",
    "page_kind": "caption",
    "status": "image_checked_draft",
    "ocr_raw": "豬屎犵狫，石阡、黎平二府，平遠州、清平縣皆有之。自面經年不潔，所居穢而不遜，與犬豕同牢。呼獸即呼食，如狼出入。佩刀弩，有讎必報。若將力□不能，則備牛酒以款。□力者即片肉卮酒□甘心報之。在清平者通漢語，聽約束。",
    "transcription": "豬屎犵狫，石阡、黎平二府，平遠州、清平縣皆有之。自面經年不潔，所居穢而不遜，與犬豕同牢。呼獸即呼食，如狼出入。佩刀弩，有讎必報。若將力□不能，則備牛酒以款。□力者即片肉卮酒□甘心報之。在清平者通漢語，聽約束。",
    "translation": "The Pig-Dung Gelao live in both Shiqian and Liping prefectures, as well as Pingyuan Prefecture and Qingping County. The compiler claims that they leave their faces unwashed for years, that their homes are filthy and disorderly, and that they live together with dogs and pigs. [The phrase “calling animals is calling food” is uncertain.] They come and go like wolves. They wear knives and crossbows and are said to avenge every feud. If their strength is [unreadable] insufficient, they prepare cattle and wine to entertain [others]. [A largely unclear clause mentions a strong person, a piece of meat, a cup of wine, and taking revenge.] Those living in Qingping speak Chinese and accept official regulation.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft",
    "transcription_scope": "full_page_visual_pass_provisional_with_uncertain_characters",
    "text_completeness": "provisional_full_or_near_full",
    "english_completeness": "full_relative_to_chinese_draft",
    "translation_scope": "Full English rendering of extant provisional Chinese transcription in source order; unreadable OCR retained, not a human-certified translation.",
    "ocr_engine": "AI-assisted visual manuscript reading, not automated OCR",
    "source_review": "User-uploaded Manchester Chinese Crawford 398 IIIF canvas 113 (source checked 2026-11-22)",
    "notes": "依上傳曼徹斯特本原圖按竪排自右向左試錄；存疑字保留□；非人工定稿，歷史族稱反映原編繪者立場。 英文為試譯性概述，並非逐字對譯。",
    "english_kind": "source_aligned_translation_draft",
    "prior_english_summary_20261122": "The historical description places this group in Shiqian, Liping, Pingyuan and Qingping. In disparaging language it describes poor hygiene, proximity to animals and the carrying of weapons; it also refers to feuds, hospitality involving cattle and wine, and Chinese-speaking communities in Qingping. Several characters in the middle of the passage remain unclear.",
    "translation_full_draft_20261122": true
  },
